Tweety is a robin.
    [1/2] tell   _robin_n_1(tweety)
Every robin is a bird.
    [1/2] rule   _bird_n_1(X) :- _robin_n_1(X)
Every bird flies.
    [2/2] rule   _fly_v_1(E!, X) :- _bird_n_1(X)
Birds are animals.
    [1/1] rule   _animal_n_1(X) :- _bird_n_1(X)
Tweety sings.
    [1/1] tell   _sing_v_1(e1, tweety)
Every robin that sings is happy.
    [1/5] rule   _happy_a_with(E!, X) :- _robin_n_1(X), _sing_v_1(E1, X)
Kim is a person.
    [1/1] tell   _person_n_1(kim)
Kim owns a dog.
    [1/1] tell   _own_v_1(e1, kim, a1)
    tell   _dog_n_1(a1)
Every person who owns a dog is happy.
    [1/1] rule   _happy_a_with(E!, X) :- _person_n_1(X), _own_v_1(E1, X, Y), _dog_n_1(Y)
Kim likes Sandy.
    [1/1] tell   _like_v_1(e1, kim, sandy)
Rex is a dog.
    [1/1] tell   _dog_n_1(rex)
Every dog has an owner.
    [1/1] rule   _have_v_1(E!, X, Y!), _owner_n_of(Y) :- _dog_n_1(X)
Does Tweety fly?
    [1/5] ask    _fly_v_1(E, tweety)
Is Tweety a bird?
    [1/5] ask    _bird_n_1(tweety)
Is Tweety an animal?
    [1/5] ask    _animal_n_1(tweety)
Is Tweety happy?
    [1/5] ask    _happy_a_with(E, tweety)
What flies?
    [1/3] ask X  _fly_v_1(E, X)
Which robin sings?
    [1/2] ask X  _robin_n_1(X), _sing_v_1(E, X)
Who is happy?
    [1/1] ask X  _happy_a_with(E, X)
Who likes Sandy?
    [1/1] ask X  _like_v_1(E, X, sandy)
Who does Kim like?
    [1/2] ask X  _like_v_1(E, kim, X)
Is Sandy happy?
    [1/5] ask    _happy_a_with(E, sandy)
Does Rex have an owner?
    [1/2] ask    _have_v_1(E, rex, X), _owner_n_of(X)
Pingu is a penguin.
